WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.965
Express applications consume CPU, memory,
database capacity,

2
00:00:05.045 --> 00:00:10.833
third-party API quota, email and SMS
budget, storage, and operational attention.

3
00:00:10.913 --> 00:00:15.875
Abuse controls protect availability, cost,
and sensitive workflows.

4
00:00:15.955 --> 00:00:20.503
Rate limits, request size limits,
pagination limits, timeouts,

5
00:00:20.583 --> 00:00:26.702
concurrency controls, and
endpoint-specific throttles should reflect the risk of the

6
00:00:26.782 --> 00:00:27.278
route.

7
00:00:27.358 --> 00:00:33.311
Login, password reset, signup, invitation,
checkout, export, webhook processing,

8
00:00:33.391 --> 00:00:39.345
and expensive search often need stronger
controls than simple read-only endpoints.

9
00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:45.296
Logging should make the application
operable without turning logs into a sensitive

10
00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:46.203
data store.

11
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:49.756
Useful logs may include request IDs, route
names,

12
00:00:49.836 --> 00:00:54.880
authenticated identity or service
identity, authorization failures,

13
00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.755
validation failures, security events,
response outcomes, timing,

14
00:00:59.835 --> 00:01:01.489
and dependency errors.

15
00:01:01.569 --> 00:01:06.944
Logs should avoid passwords, secrets,
bearer tokens, session identifiers,

16
00:01:07.024 --> 00:01:12.481
full cookies, reset links, private
payloads, and unnecessary personal data.

17
00:01:12.561 --> 00:01:17.027
Redaction and retention decisions should
be part of the design.

18
00:01:17.107 --> 00:01:22.895
Monitoring and incident response depend on
useful evidence before something goes

19
00:01:22.975 --> 00:01:23.471
wrong.

20
00:01:23.551 --> 00:01:28.430
Teams should watch for unusual traffic
patterns, repeated failures,

21
00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:32.892
high error rates, authorization denials,
validation spikes,

22
00:01:32.972 --> 00:01:37.024
dependency failures, suspicious use of
sensitive flows,

23
00:01:37.104 --> 00:01:40.246
and sudden changes in resource
consumption.

24
00:01:40.326 --> 00:01:44.708
If the team cannot tell who accessed what,
which checks failed,

25
00:01:44.788 --> 00:01:48.427
or why traffic spiked, response becomes
guesswork.

26
00:01:48.507 --> 00:01:53.220
Secure Express applications are not only
coded; they are operated.
